# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
dist/
build/

# Tooling state
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Stamped into the wheel at build time from `git log`, so committing it would be
# committing a number that is wrong by the next commit. See `about.stamp()`.
src/targum/activity.json

# Runtime output and secrets
targum-out/
.env
*.local.toml
# Personal launchers: they source .env, so they stay out of the repo.
serve.sh
Targum.command

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Claude Code session checkpoints
.claude/

# Filled-in copy of targum.env.example, for scp to the box. Never committed.
deploy/targum.env
# The catalogue is private data; it never enters the repository.
catalogue.json
!tests/fixtures/catalogue.json

# The half of the weekly that *makes* an issue. Content targum creates is proprietary,
# and so is the code that creates it — the same split `catalogue.py` already makes,
# where the types and the loader are open and the data is not. Two consequences worth
# knowing: hatchling leaves VCS-ignored files out of the wheel, so none of this ships
# and it has to run from a working tree; and running it off the server rather than on
# it keeps the generator clear of AGPL section 13. Everything that *serves* an issue —
# models, index, entries, verify, the routes and the templates — stays in the repo.
src/targum/weekly/sources.py
src/targum/weekly/facts.py
src/targum/weekly/prompts.py
src/targum/weekly/write.py
# Issues themselves live under targum-out/, already ignored above.

# The half of a dialogue that *writes* one. Same split, same reason: the scenes are
# content and so is the code that composes and voices them. Everything that *serves* a
# dialogue — the models, the index, the fetcher, the reader — stays in the repo, so a
# checkout can open a scene it is given and cannot invent one.
src/targum/dialogue/write.py
src/targum/dialogue/voice.py
src/targum/dialogue/shelf.py
# The scenes themselves live under targum-out/dialogues/, already ignored above.
#
# `feeds.py` and `verify.py` are deliberately NOT here. Parsing somebody else's XML is
# not content and not a moat, and `verify.py` holds the licence guard and the band gate
# — the two things that stop this doing harm. Private, both would sit where CI can never
# run them, which is the worst home for the code that enforces a licence boundary.

# Content, and the code that makes it: a recording is somebody else's reading of a
# text, cut and aligned here and never committed.
src/targum/recording/cut.py
src/targum/weekly/voice.py
